well black friday typically kicks off  the holiday shopping season this year  the pandemic forced many retailers to  adjust their plans to avoid any large  crowds  eight news now reporter kate houston  spoke to some shoppers to see what they  thought of the changes  although the pandemic is changing how  people shop golden knights fans showed  out to city national arena  to safely score some black friday deals  instead of long lines and early crowds  many retailers saw a slower stream of  holiday shoppers this black friday  due to health and safety protocols  stores like the arsenal at city national  arena  only led a certain amount of shoppers in  at a time to keep everyone in the  holiday spirit  the arsenal brought in live music and  games for those that shopped in person  it's different but i couldn't just stay  inside it's tradition you've got to go  out and go shopping  it's way better i think the social  distancing part of it  so people aren't right behind you  instead of doorbuster deals many big box  stores promoted online deals and offered  curbside pickup some shoppers tell me  social distancing protocols  weren't necessary since there were so  few people inside  it's probably the weirdest black friday  shopping ever like  you know usually these lines are out the  doors around the corner today they  walked right in and didn't have to do  anything  due to the financial hardships of kovid  19 the nevada retail association  projects sales at brick and mortar  locations  will go down between 3 to 8 percent from  last year  however analysts expect online sales  will help fill in that gap  kate houston 8 news now
 

